{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c383","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0b194-ebfd-7972-8291-a2a8c88f871f"},"sequence":28,"recorded_at":1780531400000051,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"df48a3ca-89bc-4d9a-855b-51fcacb1bf2e","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"df48a3ca-89bc-4d9a-855b-51fcacb1bf2e","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"df48a3ca-89bc-4d9a-855b-51fcacb1bf2e"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0b195-0947-7161-a35c-7f50f247d35b"},"task_id":"01a0b195-0947-7161-a35c-7f50f247d35b","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0b195-0947-7161-a35c-7f50f247d35b","chunk":"{\n  \"chunk_id\": \"exec-1-1\",\n  \"command\": \"task-board m 'set_status(TASK-260830-1geqhj, status=development)' 2>&1 | head -50\\necho ---\\ncat /Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/resources/TASK-260830-1geqhj/TASK-260830-1geqhj_producer.md\\necho \\\"====REWORK====\\\"\\ncat /Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/resources/TASK-260830-1geqhj/TASK-260830-1geqhj_rework-rev4.md\",\n  \"description\": \"Set status and read task files\",\n  \"exit_code\": 0,\n  \"terminal_status\": \"completed\",\n  \"output\": \"{\\\"ok\\\":true,\\\"result\\\":{\\\"primary\\\":{\\\"action\\\":\\\"status_changed\\\",\\\"element_id\\\":\\\"TASK-260830-1geqhj\\\",\\\"field\\\":\\\"status\\\",\\\"new_value\\\":\\\"development\\\",\\\"old_value\\\":\\\"to-dev\\\"},\\\"side_effects\\\":[{\\\"action\\\":\\\"escalated\\\",\\\"element_id\\\":\\\"STORY-260830-ptxkqe\\\",\\\"field\\\":\\\"status\\\",\\\"new_value\\\":\\\"development\\\",\\\"old_value\\\":\\\"to-dev\\\"},{\\\"action\\\":\\\"escalated\\\",\\\"element_id\\\":\\\"EPIC-260830-1uwj54\\\",\\\"field\\\":\\\"status\\\",\\\"new_value\\\":\\\"development\\\",\\\"old_value\\\":\\\"integrating\\\"}]}}\\n---\\nOwn TASK-260830-1geqhj (implement-ax-pane-enforcement-wrapper), first leaf of STORY-260830-ptxkqe (ax-pane-and-terminal-instance-binding, EPIC M1; siblings 2056mm and kkh1an follow). Implement, as production code over the landed owners, the validation core of the `ax pane SESSION_ID` wrapper that every managed pane runs (pinned Section 4.C wrapper rule: validate configuration, load the logical session, synchronize known lease records when possible, compare the local fencing token before launching a provider, park safely when ownership is remote, ambiguous or unverified; the after-restore sequence 1-5: read the latest locally known lease, attempt a bounded mesh lease refresh, resume locally only with the winning lease AND a valid required materialization, offer remote attach/takeover when another host owns the session interactively, otherwise enter parked without launching the provider; terminal creation and the wrapper's first child start idempotent on (session_id, bootstrap_operation_id) with the pair durably bound before child creation, identical retry reattaching to the one recorded wrapper/child, changed operation in the bootstrap window = idempotency_mismatch, status plus the binding proving absence or identifying the one child after a lost result; never persist or replicate a PID; no credential-dependent tmux server creation from a background caller — capability_unavailable with typed realm/readiness details, never direct server creation; cached sentinel/managername never authorizes resume), Section 4.B (Terminal Backend Manifest/Probe identity through the landed internal/terminalbackend registry and contract), Section 4.D (closed capabilities and evidence: only the exact admitted capability rows authorize an action; no capability map invented), Section 5.2 Terminal Events (the events the wrapper authors: session.parked with its reason vocabulary, terminal binding/evidence events as the schema defines them — author them through the landed sessrepo append path under the current lease), Section 7.A (Provider Protocol 3.0.0 Terminal Instance binding descriptor: terminal_binding_id, terminal_instance_id UUIDv7 never a PID/handle, backend id/version/protocol/generation, interactive, columns/rows — the wrapper supplies it and the provider must reject a mismatched binding; the LeaseToken is passed separately) and Section 13.1 Direct-session launch steps 3-4 (create the durable terminal entry with the bootstrap operation ID, then provider launch with its validated argv/env plan). The wrapper's checks must be composed from the landed gates rather than re-implemented: owner epoch/fencing through internal/fencing (AuthorizeActivation/Input/Restore, the sealed LeaseToken projection, park vocabulary), materialization validity through internal/matjournal + internal/sessckpt admission, profile through internal/sessprofile (effective persisted profile + Section 7.7 mapping refusals, profile_mapping_unavailable), provider identity through internal/provhost (CreateIdentity/Verify*, resume tuple gate, resumesmoke evidence as a precondition input), resume authorization through the fencing gates plus the checkpoint admission. Deliver a library entry (for example internal/axpane) with a pure decision core (inputs: config, session record + chain, local lease view, materialization/journal state, profile, provider identity, terminal backend probe, realm readiness; output: exactly one of launch|reattach|attach_remote|takeover_offer|parked(reason)|refused(class)) plus the durable bootstrap binding (session_id, bootstrap_operation_id) under the landed no-replace + fsync discipline with crash hooks and a real-kill test; no `ax` CLI, no tmux/ConPTY process control, no provider process — those are modeled callers and later leaves (state the bound). Tests: every decision outcome driven with positives and the full refusal/park table (lower/losing epoch, foreign session, expired grant, absent/ambiguous/remote ownership, invalid materialization, unmapped profile, unresolved provider identity or realm, backend probe mismatch, background caller without an attested server, changed bootstrap operation in-window, lost-result reattach), the after-restore sequence as a scenario table, idempotency of the bootstrap binding, crash at the binding seam, narrowing mutants per gate composition (each composition check dropped or reordered must be caught), and a conformance matrix over 4.B/4.C/4.D/5.2/7.A/13.1 clauses.\\nCOMMON CONTRACT (first leaf of a Story on trunk 7efe385 or later). Normative authority: the pinned internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.6.0.md headings named below; the v0.7.0 adoption (STORY-260916-3uqbwq) is landing during your run and is byte-identical in these sections — if it has landed when you refresh, cite SPEC.v0.7.0.md with the same heading numbers. Reuse the landed owners (canonicaljson closed shapes, scalar, sessrepo/sessstate/sessquery, sessckpt, matjournal, crashgate, fencing, sessprofile, provhost/resumesmoke, hosttrust/peeridentity/sshtransport, rpcwire/hostchannel, sessadapter, terminalbackend, localstore, secprim/secconftest) — never fork a second model; extend or adopt. Traceability: do NOT edit internal/traceability (ownership registry, digest pin, README measured-coverage subsection) in this first leaf — the Story's FINAL leaf carries the registry bindings and re-pin once the v0.7.0 registry is on trunk; document your clause coverage in a package-local TRACEABILITY.md and the conformance matrix instead. Evidence contract (repository DoD): every AC row driven through a production entry by a named committed test with a stated n-of-m ratio and call sites; negatives for every refusal; a narrowing mutant per gate executed by a shipped harness with per-plant raw logs, subprocess exits and an applied harmless SURVIVED control (PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1, no __pycache__); crash/idempotency evidence for every durable write (landed no-replace + fsync + hooks + real-kill pattern) or an explicit purity bound; conformance matrix mapping each pinned clause to a test or a stated bound; README package section with test commands and NO capability/CLI claim (no ax command exists); LOGBOOK entry newest-first. Managed Story worktree contract: .temp/STORY-260830-ptxkqe/worktree on branch task-board/story/STORY-260830-ptxkqe forked from current trunk; leave the candidate UNCOMMITTED; never commit on the Story branch, never edit .task-board directly, never self-accept; if origin/main moves before your handoff run `task-board worktree refresh-candidate TASK-260830-1geqhj` (LOGBOOK append conflicts are resolved with --replay-resolutions: copy the retained resolution-template.json header and add {\\\"checkpoint_oid\\\": <REBASE_HEAD original checkpoint>, \\\"path\\\": \\\"LOGBOOK.md\\\", \\\"sha256\\\": <hex of the full replacement>, \\\"content\\\": <base64>} keeping every entry newest-first), then audit every path in `git diff --name-only <old-base> <new-base>` that appears in your git status and restore trunk content by hand (README/LOGBOOK purely additive; task-board.config.json == HEAD); before handoff `git status` shows only candidate paths. Read the Curator-managed go-testing-tools skill first; all CI local; run the full configured 26-command validation suite once before handoff (command 21 is `cataloggen -adopted … -check`); hand off no later than 3.5 hours in — if the full scope cannot be finished, hand off a reviewable slice with the remaining items enumerated precisely in the results. Attach TASK-260830-1geqhj_results.md, TASK-260830-1geqhj_conformance-matrix.md and TASK-260830-1geqhj_producer-evidence.tar.gz (a real gzip archive), keep the checklist current, then `task-board handoff TASK-260830-1geqhj --role developer` and exit promptly. Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board. Model: muse-spark max. The independent reviewer will be claude-opus-5 max.\\n====REWORK====\\nTHIS BRIEF SUPERSEDES EVERY EARLIER PRECONDITION ON TASK-260830-1geqhj, including TASK-260830-1geqhj_rework-rev3.md. Do exactly what is written here.\\nRework run for TASK-260830-1geqhj (implement-ax-pane-enforcement-wrapper, first leaf of STORY-260830-ptxkqe). Change Request revision 3 was reviewed by RUN-260917-19551c (claude-opus-5 max) and routed CHANGES REQUESTED: one P1 class at two sites with a single root cause, one P2, eight P3. Read TASK-260830-1geqhj_review-verdict-rev3.md and TASK-260830-1geqhj_review-evidence-rev3.tar.gz before touching code. Every other rev2 finding is graded FIXED on the reviewer's own executed evidence — the epoch-1 owner now restores and reattaches correctly, supersession keeps every pair's receipt (three receipts on disk, real-SIGKILL seams both ways), the no-checkpoint-store guard is right, the losing-epoch Emit arm and create-from-stopped are pinned, the foreground credential path and the realm-order dependence are closed. None of that is in scope now.\\nP1-1 — and again the wrong half of it is my instruction, not your judgement. My rev3 brief said \\\"a successor lease's Checkpoint is the handoff base and must AGREE with the fold — assert that\\\". You implemented it literally as `leaseCheckpointAgrees` requiring `Winner.Checkpoint == NewestCheckpointID`. As a strict equality it is wrong past the handoff instant: a successor lease's `checkpoint_id` is the handoff base at takeover time and NEVER moves (lease records are immutable and the landed sessrepo has no renewal API — only CreateLease, CompareAndSwapLease, GetLease, ListLeases, WinningLease), while every checkpoint the new owner publishes afterwards lands on the chain. So the equality holds only until the successor owner's first checkpoint, and after that every session that was ever taken over can never restore on its own owner again: the reviewer drove it through `Run` on real stores — epoch-1 owner stops with C0, a local successor lease (2,S) is minted with base C0, the successor owner appends `session.resumed`(C0) → `session.idle` → checkpointed `session.stopped`(C1), the landed reducer folds it to newest=C1, and a restore naming C1 with a journal sourced from C1 is PARKED `restore_policy` \\\"winning lease checkpoint disagrees with the newest published checkpoint\\\".\\nThe corrected rule, which is the requirement and not a mechanism to copy: the FOLD is the sole newest-checkpoint authority. The only fact a successor lease adds is the implication \\\"a checkpoint-carrying winner implies the fold has a published newest\\\" — assert exactly that, at ONE site every launch-class path passes (directly after the fencing arm, so it owns the decision for launch and restore alike, with or without a required checkpoint or journal), and park `restore_policy` when a checkpoint-carrying winner meets a null fold. Assert NOTHING else about the lease's base — binding the base to a checkpoint the chain published is the takeover leaf's obligation, so state it as an explicit bound — and delete the equality.\\nSecond site, and this is why the two must land together: `run.go:213-216` loads the profile closure from `observation.Winner.Checkpoint` whenever the winner carries one, i.e. from the handoff base rather than the checkpoint actually resumed. The reviewer proved the dangerous direction on the exact tree: C0's closure says `yolo`, the successor owner set the profile back to `standard` before C1, and the launch carries **`yolo` with mapping `--dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox`** on a session whose authoritative profile is `standard`. It also proved that relaxing the agreement ALONE turns the park into exactly that wrong-profile launch. So: derive the profile closure from the checkpoint actually resumed — the required checkpoint when one is required; otherwise the fold's newest, loaded through `sessckpt` by that ID, when the fold has one; otherwise the session head (the epoch-1 bootstrap-retry path) — never `Winner.Checkpoint`. Re-pin: the post-takeover lifecycle positive (successor owner published C1, restore launches with C1's closure profile) through both `Decide` and `Run`; the stale-base negative; the successor-with-null-fold negative; and the wrong-profile plants in BOTH directions (`yolo`→`standard` and `standard`→`yolo`) through `Run`. Ship a narrowing mutant on the null-fold conditional and one on the closure source — a mutant that reads `Winner.Checkpoint` again must die.\\nP2-1 — post-window same-pair race through `Run` reports `launch` with the winner's child. `Store.Supersede` replays the winner's receipt on the link `EEXIST` but returns no replay signal, and run.go:284-289 reports the decision unchanged: with a concurrent wrapper recording the same new pair between stage and link, the loser's outcome is `launch` with the WINNER's terminal instance while its own admitted descriptor names a different one. The in-window `Bind` path already flips this race to `reattach`; the post-window path must too. Make `Supersede` report replay (or compare the returned instance with the candidate), flip the outcome to `ActionReattach` with the recorded receipt, pin it through `Run` with the `Hooks.AfterStage` hook, and ship a narrowing mutant (a replay that keeps `launch`).\\nP3 — eight items, each named in the verdict with its probe or plant. Work them all, and note P3-3: the acceptance table has 43 rows and you publish 43/43, but the reviewer measures 39 of 43 because the four rows touching the post-takeover lifecycle are pinned to a refusal the spec does not require — once P1-1 is fixed those rows change meaning, so re-derive the ratio on the fixed tree and publish the number you measure.\\nAlso correct the prose the fix invalidates: the README sentence \\\"Resume derives the effective profile from the validated checkpoint's event-head closure\\\", the rev3 LOGBOOK F1 sentence \\\"a successor lease's handoff base must agree with the fold\\\", and the matching TRACEABILITY rows.\\nProcedure: your candidate is on base 2fc6d5074058ab7970c87982deac50b3e3f5fd91, which is current origin/main — do not refresh unless it actually moves; if it does, run `task-board worktree refresh-candidate TAS